# Callback Props

Props send data **down**: a parent writes an attribute, a child reads it. The last reading left us needing the opposite. State lives in a common ancestor, and a click three levels down has to change it.

The answer is still props. The trick is that **a prop can hold a function**.

## The Idea

The parent writes a function and passes it as a prop. The child, when something happens, calls it.

```text
Parent ---- clicked={handleClick} ----> Child      (a function goes down)
Parent <--- handleClick() called ------ Child      (a call comes back up)
```

Nothing new is being introduced. A prop's value can be a string, a number, an object, or a function, and a function is the useful case here because the child can *call* it.

## A Child Reporting a Click

```{react autorun}
function ChatInput({ clicked }) {
  return (
    <div>
      <input placeholder="Type a message" />
      <button onClick={() => clicked()}>Send</button>
    </div>
  );
}

function App() {
  function handleSend() {
    console.log('The parent knows the child button was clicked.');
  }

  return <ChatInput clicked={handleSend} />;
}

renderReact(<App />);
```

Click Send and read the console. `handleSend` is defined in `App` and runs because `ChatInput` called it.

Follow the path once, because it is the whole pattern:

1. `App` defines `handleSend`.
2. `App` passes it down as the `clicked` prop.
3. `ChatInput` puts its own handler on the button's `onClick`.
4. That handler calls `clicked()`, which is `handleSend`, back up in `App`.

The child does not know what happens next, and does not need to. It reports; the parent decides.

## Sending Data, Not Only Events

A callback takes arguments like any function, so the child can pass information along with the news:

```{react autorun}
function ItemButton({ item, onChoose }) {
  return <button onClick={() => onChoose(item)}>{item}</button>;
}

function App() {
  const [selected, setSelected] = React.useState('None');

  return (
    <section>
      <ItemButton item="Apple" onChoose={setSelected} />
      <ItemButton item="Grape" onChoose={setSelected} />
      <ItemButton item="Pear" onChoose={setSelected} />

      <p>Selected: {selected}</p>
    </section>
  );
}

renderReact(<App />);
```

Each button reports *which* item was chosen. Note what `App` passed as `onChoose`: `setSelected`, the state setter itself. A setter is an ordinary function, so it can be handed down as a callback prop directly, with no wrapper.

And notice where the state is. `selected` belongs to `App`, not to the buttons. The buttons cannot change it and do not try to; they call a function and `App` does the changing. That keeps one component in charge of one piece of state, however many children can trigger a change.

## Naming

Handler props are conventionally named for the event they report, from the parent's point of view: `onChoose`, `onSend`, `onDelete`, `onValueChange`. Naming them for the child's internal mechanics, like `buttonClicked`, ties the parent to how the child happens to be built today.

The `on` prefix here is convention, not machinery. For a real DOM element, `onClick` is understood by React; for your own component, `onChoose` is a prop name like any other, and it means whatever the child does with it.

## Exercises

1. Wire the callback so clicking the child's button tells the parent.

   ```{react}
   function SendButton({ onSend }) {
     // TODO: call onSend when the button is clicked
     return <button>Send</button>;
   }

   function App() {
     const [sends, setSends] = React.useState(0);

     function handleSend() {
       setSends(sends + 1);
     }

     return (
       <section>
         <SendButton onSend={handleSend} />
         <p>Sent {sends} times</p>
       </section>
     );
   }

   renderReact(<App />);
   ```
   ```+solution
   function SendButton({ onSend }) {
     return <button onClick={() => onSend()}>Send</button>;
   }

   function App() {
     const [sends, setSends] = React.useState(0);

     function handleSend() {
       setSends(sends + 1);
     }

     return (
       <section>
         <SendButton onSend={handleSend} />
         <p>Sent {sends} times</p>
       </section>
     );
   }

   renderReact(<App />);
   ```
   ```+test
   // React commits its render asynchronously, so the assertions below run
   // on the next tick. Asserting immediately would find an empty container.
   setTimeout(function () {
     assert('p').should('have.text', 'Sent 0 times').run('The count should start at zero', 'Starts at zero');
     assert('button').click().run('Click Send', 'Clicked Send');
     // The state update re-renders asynchronously too, so give it a tick.
     setTimeout(function () {
       assert('p').should('have.text', 'Sent 1 times').run('Clicking the child`s button should run the parent`s `onSend` callback', 'The parent heard the click');
     }, 50);
   }, 50);
   ```

2. Pass data up as well as the event: each button should report its own label.

   ```{react}
   function ItemButton({ item, onChoose }) {
     // TODO: call onChoose with this button's item when it is clicked
     return <button>{item}</button>;
   }

   function App() {
     const [selected, setSelected] = React.useState('None');

     return (
       <section>
         <ItemButton item="Apple" onChoose={setSelected} />
         <ItemButton item="Grape" onChoose={setSelected} />

         <p>Selected: {selected}</p>
       </section>
     );
   }

   renderReact(<App />);
   ```
   ```+solution
   function ItemButton({ item, onChoose }) {
     return <button onClick={() => onChoose(item)}>{item}</button>;
   }

   function App() {
     const [selected, setSelected] = React.useState('None');

     return (
       <section>
         <ItemButton item="Apple" onChoose={setSelected} />
         <ItemButton item="Grape" onChoose={setSelected} />

         <p>Selected: {selected}</p>
       </section>
     );
   }

   renderReact(<App />);
   ```
   ```+test
   setTimeout(function () {
     assert('p').should('have.text', 'Selected: None').run('Nothing should be selected at the start', 'Nothing selected yet');
     wrap(document.querySelectorAll('button')[1].textContent).should('equal', 'Grape').run('Both item buttons should render, labelled from their `item` prop', 'Both buttons render');
     document.querySelectorAll('button')[1].click();
     setTimeout(function () {
       assert('p').should('have.text', 'Selected: Grape').run('Call `onChoose(item)` so the parent learns which button was clicked, not only that one was', 'Reported which item was chosen');
     }, 50);
   }, 50);
   ```

3. How does a child send information to its parent?

   ```{mcq}
   Props go from parent to child. What carries information the other way?
   correct: Correct! The parent passes a function down as a prop, and the child calls it. Arguments to that call are how data travels up.
   wrong: Nothing new is added for this. Think about what kinds of values a prop is allowed to hold.
   [x] The parent passes a function as a prop, and the child calls it
   [ ] The child sets a prop on its parent
   [ ] The child modifies the prop object it received
   [ ] React copies child state up automatically
   ```

4. Why does the state stay in the parent?

   ```{mcq}
   Three buttons can all change the selection. Why does `selected` live in `App` rather than in the buttons?
   correct: Correct! One piece of state in one place, changed through a callback by whichever child needs to. If each button held its own, there would be three selections and no single answer to what is selected.
   wrong: Ask what "selected" would even mean if each of the three buttons kept its own copy.
   [x] There is one selection, so it needs one owner that all three buttons report to
   [ ] Children are not allowed to call `useState`
   [ ] Callback props only work when the parent holds the state
   [ ] It makes the buttons render faster
   ```

